  <static seed="485" source="Library/Rochester/setVmultivariable6Gradient/ur_vc_6_15.pg">
    <statement><p>Suppose that distances are measured in lightyears and that the
temperature T of a gaseous nebula is inversely proportional to the
distance from a fixed point, which we take to be the origin. Suppose
that the temperature 1 lightyear from the origin is 800 degrees
celsius. Find the gradient of T at <m>\left( x, y, z \right)</m>.</p>
<p><m>\nabla T = (</m> <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0001"/>, <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0002"/>,  <fillin characters="25" name="AnSwEr0003"/> <m>)</m></p>
<p>Note: Your answers should be expressions of x, y and z; e.g. <lq/>3x - 4yz<rq/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{-800x}{\left(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}\right)^{1.5}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{-800y}{\left(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}\right)^{1.5}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\frac{-800z}{\left(x^{2}+y^{2}+z^{2}\right)^{1.5}}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
  </static>
